---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="About" description="Who builds this notebook, and why their engineering perspective is worth your time.">
	<div class="prose">
		<p class="kicker">About</p>
		<h1>Build things. Write down why.</h1>
		<p>
			I'm a production data engineer. For years my work was pipelines, migrations,
			and the 3 AM pages that teach you what "done" really means. Somewhere along
			the way I became a systems builder by habit: if I solve something twice, I
			extract the pattern; if a decision matters, I write down why before the
			reasons evaporate.
		</p>
		<p>
			This notebook is where that habit went independent. The projects here are
			mine — a study platform, a wealth tracker, a mutual-fund intelligence
			system, the tooling around them — built outside any employer's roadmap,
			held to a standard I'd defend in any review: tested, measured, and honest
			about what broke.
		</p>
		<p>
			Lately the work changed shape again. AI writes most first drafts now,
			including some of the code on this site. So the notebook changed with it:
			less "here is code," more "here is the reasoning the code came from" —
			constraints, rejected alternatives, verification, and the failures kept on
			record. Implementation is cheap. Judgment is the scarce part, and this is
			where I practice it in public.
		</p>
		<h2>What I work on</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>Data engineering — Spark, Databricks, pipelines, migrations</li>
			<li>AI and agents — LLM systems, memory, evaluation, tooling</li>
			<li>Systems and architecture — storage, sync, offline-first design</li>
			<li>Developer tools — scanners, notebooks, automation that compounds</li>
		</ul>
		<h2>How I work, in one page</h2>
		<p>
			Every project here follows the same discipline: reason first, then build,
			then measure. Decisions get written down with their alternatives. Tests
			pin the math. Metrics come from runs, not adjectives — and where something
			isn't measured, the page says so. If you want the method stated plainly,
			start with any project page and read its struggle section first.
		</p>
		<h2>Elsewhere</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<a href="https://github.com/xmirtunjay" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href="/engineering-notebook/rss.xml">RSS</a>
			</li>
		</ul>
		<h2>How this site is built</h2>
		<p>
			Astro, static generation, GitHub Pages. Content lives in typed collections; case studies
			are generated from repository evidence, never invented. The full inventory pipeline and
			content tooling are part of the story, not hidden behind it.
		</p>
		<p>
			Performance budget, measured from the production build: homepage 19 KB HTML, heaviest
			page 42 KB HTML (a tutorial with diagrams), all images lazy-loaded, Mermaid and its diagram dependencies
			loaded on demand only on pages that contain diagrams. One demo video (1 MB) is the
			largest asset on the site; everything else is text.
		</p>
	</div>
</Base>

